INT. HARBOR ROAD VETERINARY CLINIC - WAITING ROOM - DAY

MO
(behind the desk, not looking up from the screen)
Mrs. K's beagle is in room two. Dr. Sam says give her ten minutes.

THE WOMAN
(standing at the counter, a cat carrier held tight against her chest)
I don't have ten minutes. I have a cat who hasn't eaten since Sunday and a boss who thinks I'm at a dentist.

MO
(beat)
I'm sorry. I really am. But it's triage. Whoever's worst goes first.

THE WOMAN
(setting the carrier down very carefully)
He is the worst. Look at him. Look. (beat) You're not looking.

MO
(looking now, softer)
Okay. Okay, he's not great. Give me one second.

SAM
(coming through the swinging door, drying her hands on her scrubs)
Mo, who's shouting?

MO
(not turning)
Nobody's shouting. Yet.

THE WOMAN
(to Sam, quick)
Are you the doctor? Please. I'll sign anything. I'll wait in the car. Just look at him.

SAM
(kneeling by the carrier, a long breath)
Hi there, buddy. Hi. (beat) What's his name?

THE WOMAN
(after a moment, quieter)
Biscuit. He was my husband's. Ex-husband's. He left him. He left the cat and not me, which, I mean, tells you something.

SAM
(a small smile, still watching the cat)
It tells me he was an idiot.

MO
(quietly, to no one)
Wow. Okay.

SAM
(standing, looking at Mo)
Don't start.

MO
(finally turning)
I'm not starting. I'm just saying you've been in this clinic eleven years and you've never once said "idiot" about a client's ex.

THE WOMAN
(to Mo, surprised)
Are you two--

MO
(flat)
No.

SAM
(at the same time)
We were.

MO
(sharp)
Sam.

SAM
(to the Woman, gentle)
Her name's Jen, right? On the intake form. Jen, if you come through, I'll see Biscuit first. Mrs. K can wait.

JEN
(picking up the carrier, hesitating)
Thank you. I didn't mean to cause--

MO
(cutting in)
You didn't. She did.

SAM
(turning on Mo, low)
Mo. Not here.

MO
(louder)
Then where? In the supply closet, like last March? On the phone, at two a.m., like last June?

JEN
(awkward, edging back)
I can wait outside, really--

SAM
(not looking away from Mo)
Stay, Jen. Please. (beat) Mo, I was trying to be kind to her.

MO
(laughing, not a happy laugh)
You were trying to be kind to HER. That's perfect. You've got infinite kindness for strangers and animals and nothing left over for the person who actually--

BIG DAVE
(pushing the front door open with his shoulder, a golden retriever on a short lead, a bandaged paw held up)
Hey. Hey, folks. Sorry, is this a bad time? Roscoe cut himself on a bottle. It's not an emergency, he's just being dramatic.

MO
(turning, composing her face with visible effort)
Hi, Dave. No. It's fine. It's a great time.

BIG DAVE
(looking from Mo to Sam to Jen)
Is it? Because it feels like I walked in on something.

JEN
(to Big Dave, helpless)
I just came in with a cat.

BIG DAVE
(nodding slowly, sitting on the bench)
Sure. Sure you did.

SAM
(to Mo, quieter now)
I should have said something. Last spring. Before it got to this.

MO
(her voice cracking)
You should have said something in April. In May. At my sister's wedding, when you danced with me and then drove home alone.

SAM
(a step closer)
I was scared.

MO
(over her)
You're not scared of anything. You put your arm inside a horse.

SAM
(over Mo, desperate)
A horse doesn't look at me like you do.

JEN
(whispering to Big Dave)
Should we go?

BIG DAVE
(whispering back, not moving)
Absolutely not.

MO
(a long beat, then, very still)
Say it again.

SAM
(beat)
A horse doesn't look at me like you do.

MO
(quietly)
No. The other thing. The first thing.

SAM
(beat)
I was scared. I'm still scared. I'm scared right now.

MO
(a breath, then a laugh that breaks into something else)
God. You are such an idiot.

SAM
(wiping her eyes with the back of her wrist)
That's what I said about him.

BIG DAVE
(to Jen, soft)
This is better than the cat.

JEN
(a startled laugh, then a nod)
It really is.

MO
(coming around the desk at last)
Go see Biscuit. Both of you. I'll hold the front.

SAM
(not moving)
And after?

MO
(beat)
After, you buy me a coffee and you say all of it again. In daylight. Where nobody's watching.

BIG DAVE
(raising his hand)
I'm watching.

MO
(not turning)
Dave.

BIG DAVE
(lowering it)
Right. Sorry. Roscoe and I will be over here. Being dramatic.
